# What goes into the source distribution.
#
# The policy is **the complete tests directory or nothing**, and it is the
# complete directory. setuptools' default sdist picks up `tests/test_*.py` by
# a filename heuristic and left out `tests/synthetic.py`, which every one of
# those files imports — so an unpacked sdist collected ten errors reading
#
#     ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'synthetic'
#
# A half-shipped test suite is worse than none: it looks runnable, fails for a
# reason that has nothing to do with the code, and teaches a packager to stop
# running it. `graft tests` ships the fixture builder and anything added
# beside it later, so the same omission cannot recur by someone adding a
# helper with a name the heuristic does not like.
#
# Shipping them at all is deliberate: the fixtures are generated at runtime and
# no device data exists in this repository, so the tests carry nothing private
# and let a downstream packager verify the build they are about to install.
graft tests
graft docs

# `graft scripts`, not `include scripts/pre-commit`. Naming one file is how
# this went wrong twice: the sdist first shipped tests/test_*.py without
# tests/synthetic.py that every one of them imports, and then shipped
# tests/test_release_history.py without the script it tests. Both times the
# suite looked runnable and failed for a reason unrelated to the code, which
# is how a packager learns to stop running it. Enumerate the directory, so
# adding a file cannot repeat it.
graft scripts

include LICENSE
include CHANGELOG.md CONTRIBUTING.md SECURITY.md

global-exclude __pycache__
global-exclude *.py[cod]
global-exclude .DS_Store
